WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:01.870
- [James] Thanks to Audible for keeping

00:00:01.870 --> 00:00:04.090
Legal Eagle in the air.

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:05.520
- Don't call me a liar.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.430
- Oh you're a liar all right.
- Mr. Jones.

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:07.807
- You son of a (bleep)

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:10.400
(yelling)

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:12.483
- And the bailiff tackled them.

00:00:12.483 --> 00:00:15.150
(playful music)

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:19.240
Hey legal eagles, it's time
to think like a lawyer.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:21.760
Today we are covering one
of my all-time favorite

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.381
legal dramas, it is The
Rainmaker, starring Matt Damon.

00:00:25.381 --> 00:00:28.760
This I think will be the first
time I have seen this movie

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:30.830
since becoming a lawyer,
I think it has been

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.689
over 10 years since I've seen this movie.

00:00:33.689 --> 00:00:35.415
- I'm nervous, real nervous.

00:00:35.415 --> 00:00:38.330
- I'm really hoping it stands
up to the test of time.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:39.780
I remember it being really good.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:42.680
So of course, be sure to comment
in the form of an objection

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:45.130
which I will either sustain or overrule,

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:46.900
and stick around until
the end of the video

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:50.810
where I give The Rainmaker
a grade for legal realism.

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:54.660
So without further ado, let's
dig in to The Rainmaker.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:56.900
- Well before we can file it the claim,

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:59.103
I need all three of you to sign this.

00:00:59.103 --> 00:01:00.503
- When is Daddy coming here?

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.930
- Well, he said he wasn't.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.370
Some days he comes in,
some days he doesn't.

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:08.040
- Well this is a contract.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.670
- What's in it?

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:12.430
- Oh the usual, it's standard language.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:15.990
It basically says that y'all
hire us to be your attorneys

00:01:17.072 --> 00:01:18.490
and we take care of the case for you.

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:21.007
We handle any expenses
that you might have,

00:01:21.007 --> 00:01:23.823
and then we get one third of any recovery.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:25.980
- All right so this is what's called

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.590
a retention agreement, which
is something you rarely see

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.670
in the movies, but it is
something that is absolutely

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:35.800
ethically required to be able
to actually represent people.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:37.950
It lays out the terms of your engagement,

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.960
and it lays out the terms of
your compensation as well.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.160
And the contract that is being signed here

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.320
is a fairly standard one for plaintiffs

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:46.660
working on contingency.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.820
The plaintiff's attorneys
will pay for the costs,

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:52.604
which can sometimes be
very, very expensive

00:01:52.604 --> 00:01:56.762
in terms of filing fees and
experts and deposition costs,

00:01:56.762 --> 00:01:59.392
and then if there is
any recovery at the end,

00:01:59.392 --> 00:02:01.760
the plaintiff's attorneys
will get one third

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:05.454
to say 40% of what is recovered.

00:02:05.454 --> 00:02:08.426
You might say that that is a lot of money,

00:02:08.426 --> 00:02:10.490
especially when you're
sometimes dealing with

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:12.840
thousands or millions of dollars,

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.070
but the plaintiffs' attorneys
take on a huge amount of risk.

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:18.720
They might be out tens
or hundreds of thousands

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.892
of dollars, or thousands of man hours

00:02:20.892 --> 00:02:24.510
to work up these cases with no guarantee

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:25.720
that they're going to get anything.

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:27.270
So that is the trade-off

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.400
when you're dealing with contingency work.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.050
- Well why does it take two
pages to say all of that?

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:34.760
- Lawyers, man, they're the worst.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:37.617
- No wonder I'm dying.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:43.020
- Donnie Ray has leukemia
and his medical insurance

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.298
denied him a bone marrow transplant,

00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:48.816
which would have provided
relief to his condition.

00:02:48.816 --> 00:02:50.710
That's the point of this law suit.

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:53.770
- For the record, my
name is Leo F. Drummond

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:57.980
of Tenley Brit, counsel for Great Benefit,

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:02.800
and I say if this young man
has passed the Bar Exam,

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.683
your Honor, let him argue the case.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:09.252
We welcome him to big-time litigation.

00:03:09.252 --> 00:03:10.230
(laughs)

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:12.303
- You have no objection, Mr. Drummond?

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:15.630
- If it please the
court, I would be honored

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:18.722
to introduce Mr. Baylor
to the practice of law

00:03:18.722 --> 00:03:20.520
in the great state of Tennessee.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.310
Judge, you can give him the oath right now

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:25.423
and I'll be pleased to stand for him.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.330
- That's actually not just a formality.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:31.203
Usually in order to stand
for the oath of office,

00:03:31.203 --> 00:03:35.090
you have to be sponsored
by a practicing attorney.

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:36.953
This is such a good scene because you have

00:03:36.953 --> 00:03:41.610
this big firm lawyer who knows
he's getting a huge advantage

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:43.847
by getting this greenhorn attorney,

00:03:43.847 --> 00:03:46.320
and rather than getting a continuance

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.000
or dealing with another
more seasoned attorney,

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.548
he is offering to sponsor this young man

00:03:52.548 --> 00:03:55.640
to take the oath of office
so that he can, you know,

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:57.590
litigate against someone that

00:03:57.590 --> 00:03:58.680
doesn't know what they're doing.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:01.090
Kudos to John Grisham for writing this

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:03.760
and kudos to Francis Ford
Coppola for the way that

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:05.633
they staged it, it's so good.

00:04:06.482 --> 00:04:09.470
- Mr. Drummond, you objected to

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:11.263
the fast tracking of this case.

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:13.660
What's the problem?

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:16.860
- Well your Honor, this
issue's already been

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:19.253
ruled upon by Judge Hale.

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:23.672
The preparations required
fast tracking the case

00:04:23.672 --> 00:04:26.683
place an undue burden on
both parties, I believe.

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:28.700
- Nonsense.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:30.050
- Nice.

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:32.185
- Let me ask you something, Mr. Drummond.

00:04:32.185 --> 00:04:35.670
As a defense lawyer, have you ever agreed

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:38.130
to the fast tracking of a law suit?

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:40.293
- Well your Honor, I believe I have.

00:04:40.293 --> 00:04:43.621
- (laughs) Fine, give
me the name of the case

00:04:43.621 --> 00:04:45.570
and the court it was in.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:47.780
- Fast tracking, by the
way, is a thing that exists

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:49.970
in lots of different jurisdictions.

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:51.900
It doesn't have a consistent meaning,

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:54.290
it depends on what
particular court you're in,

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:56.980
but often cases will have different tracks

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:58.820
based on how complex they are,

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:02.700
and the need for any due process here,

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:05.860
and given that there is
a young man who is dying

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:08.020
of leukemia here, you can
imagine that fast tracking

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:10.380
would be very, very important to make sure

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:12.460
that all the necessary discovery is taken

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:14.110
before he passes away.

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:18.430
- How about next Thursday afternoon?

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:19.780
- Great for me, your Honor.

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:23.097
- I'm sorry, your Honor.

00:05:23.097 --> 00:05:24.980
- That's a week from today.

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:26.423
- I believe I'm out of town.

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:29.293
Yes, I am out of town Thursday.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:35.970
- Deposition is set for
next Thursday afternoon

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:40.607
at two PM, sorry if it
inconveniences the defense,

00:05:40.607 --> 00:05:44.355
but god knows there's enough
of you guys to handle it.

00:05:44.355 --> 00:05:45.670
(laughs)

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:46.919
Now this next--

00:05:46.919 --> 00:05:51.190
- So I have actually seen
a judge make a statement

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:52.690
almost identical to that.

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:56.123
It is one of the disadvantages
of showing up to court

00:05:56.123 --> 00:05:58.290
with a whole army of attorneys,

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:00.000
sometimes I've been on
the side of the army

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.620
and sometimes I've been on
the side of just one person,

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:04.990
but when you show up with
multiple people in court

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:07.400
and you claim that your
schedule doesn't allow you

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.380
to participate in certain things,

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:12.000
the judge will just look
at all of the other people

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.187
that are at counsel table and say

00:06:13.187 --> 00:06:15.657
"well if you're not
available, then one of these

00:06:15.657 --> 00:06:17.800
"other people will be
available to take care of it,"

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:20.410
so that's one of the
issues when you show up

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:22.360
with a whole bunch of other co-counsel.

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:24.760
- [Rudy] I'm looking at a team of lawyers

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:28.600
who are pulling down $1,000
an hour, and I hate them.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:31.330
From their lofty perch pissing down

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:33.423
on the whole justice system with disdain.

00:06:34.417 --> 00:06:37.317
I used to hate them because I
wasn't good enough for them.

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:40.937
Now I hate them for who they represent

00:06:40.937 --> 00:06:43.040
and what they represent.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:44.720
- [Man] Your Honor, how are you?

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:47.727
- Fine sir, hope the
dogs didn't startle you.

00:06:47.727 --> 00:06:48.560
- We'll do this thing outside.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:52.060
- It's a little cramped
inside, just have a seat here.

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:55.910
- So it would be very unusual
to hold a deposition outside,

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:58.360
just because you know,
you're in the elements

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:01.780
and it's hard to do, but it
would not be unusual at all

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:05.610
to have a whole bunch of
attorneys on both sides to

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:09.300
participate in one of the
central depositions in the case,

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.610
especially when you're
dealing with toxic torts,

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:14.110
where you have lots and
lots of different companies

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:16.430
and they all have counsel,
you might have 30 people

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:19.081
in a room participating in the deposition.

00:07:19.081 --> 00:07:21.581
(tense music)

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:25.450
- Hey Donnie Ray.

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:28.850
- Hey.

00:07:28.850 --> 00:07:30.950
- [Rudy] You've already met Judge Kippler.

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:34.350
- You would also definitely want to record

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:37.460
the deposition testimony
with a video camera,

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:38.750
as they are doing here.

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:40.240
There is an added cost, of course,

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:42.920
but it is so important to be able to see

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.190
the intonation and the facial expressions

00:07:45.190 --> 00:07:46.670
of these important witnesses

00:07:46.670 --> 00:07:48.600
when they're giving their
deposition testimony.

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:50.960
What is really, really unusual here

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:54.480
is that the judge is
participating in the deposition,

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:56.730
sort of as an observer.

00:07:56.730 --> 00:07:58.650
Often you have discovery disputes

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:00.360
that happen during the deposition,

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.974
where you threaten to call up the judge

00:08:02.974 --> 00:08:05.930
to adjudicate the matter
so that you don't have to

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:08.550
stop the deposition and
pick it up days later.

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:11.410
I guess it could be the case where

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:14.220
the judge just wants to
stick it to the other side,

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:17.470
so the judge actually
participates in deposition itself.

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:21.090
I have never seen that, but
it theoretically could happen.

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:22.630
- A young Rudy Baylor,

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:24.053
and just on time too.

00:08:24.053 --> 00:08:24.886
- Mr. Young.

00:08:24.886 --> 00:08:26.659
- Get this boy some coffee, Tyler.

00:08:26.659 --> 00:08:28.677
- Jack Underhall.
- Rudy Baylor.

00:08:28.677 --> 00:08:29.510
- Nice to meet you.

00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:30.950
- A young lawyer's always a hungry lawyer.

00:08:30.950 --> 00:08:31.893
All this boy's claims.

00:08:31.893 --> 00:08:33.550
- [Rudy Voiceover] There must be 100 years

00:08:33.550 --> 00:08:35.850
of legal experience
gathered around the table.

00:08:37.170 --> 00:08:39.540
My staff has flunked
the Bar exam six times.

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:41.450
- Now Rudy, don't be intimidated by

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:43.580
all these boys on this side of the table,

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:45.550
I guarantee you get them on a golf course,

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:47.083
they fold like a cheap suit.

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:54.200
- John Voight plays such a
great, pompous, big law attorney.

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:57.320
He's just taking up
all the air in the room

00:08:57.320 --> 00:09:00.443
and he's trying to dominate
his opposing counsel.

00:09:00.443 --> 00:09:04.080
He's just playing such
a jerk and it's perfect.

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:08.130
God, that is exactly what these older,

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:09.870
big firm attorneys are likely to do.

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:13.270
- I think maybe it's appropriate to start

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:17.814
with the corporate designee,
Jack Underhall here.

00:09:17.814 --> 00:09:19.193
- [Rudy] I don't think so.

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:21.540
- I beg your pardon?

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:23.670
- Well you heard me, I want to start with

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:25.520
Jackie Lemancyzk, the claims handler.

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:34.730
- I think it's best we
start with Mr. Underhall.

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:37.530
- All due respect, Mr. Drummond,

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:39.510
this is my deposition, I'm
gonna call these witnesses

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:41.540
in the order which I see fit.

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:43.690
So I'd like to start
with Jackie Lemancyzk.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:48.663
Maybe we should just go call the judge.

00:09:49.933 --> 00:09:52.140
- That's a great threat,
perfectly reasonable too.

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:53.170
You can do that.

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:55.141
- You get pugilistic
this time of the morning?

00:09:55.141 --> 00:09:56.150
(laughs)

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:59.060
- I'm not meaning to be pugilistic.

00:09:59.060 --> 00:10:01.270
- We're simply having a little problem

00:10:01.270 --> 00:10:05.023
with Jackie Lemancyzk, this Polish woman.

00:10:05.023 --> 00:10:07.323
- What sort of problem?
- Polish woman, jeez.

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:10.870
- She doesn't work here anymore.

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:13.133
- Was she fired?

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:15.203
- She resigned.

00:10:16.790 --> 00:10:17.623
She resigned.

00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:20.293
- Um, well where is she now?

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:24.233
- Well, she is no longer
working for our client,

00:10:26.156 --> 00:10:30.120
and we can't produce her as a
witness, so let's move along.

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:34.810
- That's true, if the
employee is no longer

00:10:34.810 --> 00:10:38.050
with the corporation, the
corporation is under no duty

00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:40.840
to produce that person for a deposition.

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:43.310
But you can bet that there
was probably some shenanigans

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:45.630
that led to that particular person

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:47.453
no longer working at this company.

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:52.543
- This bugging device has
got medium-grade circuitry.

00:10:55.029 --> 00:10:56.363
A weak transmitter,

00:10:58.250 --> 00:11:00.250
probably manufactured in Czechoslovakia.

00:11:03.439 --> 00:11:06.039
I don't think the cops or
the feds would place this.

00:11:08.544 --> 00:11:09.994
- Somebody else is listening.

00:11:11.016 --> 00:11:12.516
- Who else would listen to us?

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:16.100
- I got a pretty good idea.

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:19.120
- So Rudy's office got bugged.

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:22.150
For a long time, I thought
that would be completely

00:11:22.150 --> 00:11:25.960
unrealistic, but lately there have been

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:29.059
some law firms that have
gotten in really hot water,

00:11:29.059 --> 00:11:31.420
maybe not for bugging their opponents,

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:34.370
but for doing some really shady things.

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:37.340
For example, David Boies hiring an Israeli

00:11:37.340 --> 00:11:39.520
security firm on behalf
of Harvey Weinstein.

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:42.130
Nowadays, what used to be
something that would only

00:11:42.130 --> 00:11:45.900
be seen in fiction is actually
becoming more realistic.

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:49.010
That's, that's a sorry
state that we live in.

00:11:49.010 --> 00:11:50.220
- [Rudy Voiceover] The
judge gave us the names

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:52.023
of 92 potential jurors.

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:54.940
We investigated their backgrounds

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:56.810
and we rated them with
plus or minus numbers.

00:11:56.810 --> 00:11:57.643
- What do you want me to do?

00:11:57.643 --> 00:11:59.550
- All right, here we go.
- What?

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:01.580
- [Rudy] Any direct
contact would of course

00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:02.883
be a serious offense.

00:12:03.950 --> 00:12:05.090
- What are we doing?

00:12:05.090 --> 00:12:07.640
- We're gonna do this
thing, just do what I say.

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:08.473
- Okay.

00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:11.880
(phone rings)

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:14.150
- Hello?

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:16.726
- Yeah uh, Rudy Baylor please.

00:12:16.726 --> 00:12:18.309
- [Rudy] This is Rudy Baylor.

00:12:18.309 --> 00:12:21.710
- Uh, this is Billy Porter.

00:12:21.710 --> 00:12:23.360
You stopped by the shop today.

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:24.580
- Yes, Mr. Porter,

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:26.863
thank you very much for calling back.

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:29.690
- But what do you want?

00:12:29.690 --> 00:12:31.980
- [Rudy] Yes, it's about
the trial, you know,

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:33.910
the one you got a jury summons for?

00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:35.030
I'm one of the lawyers.

00:12:35.030 --> 00:12:37.283
- Uh-huh, is this legal?

00:12:38.510 --> 00:12:40.150
- Uh, of course it's legal, Mr. Porter,

00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:42.750
just don't go telling anybody.

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:44.020
(laughs)

00:12:44.020 --> 00:12:46.310
- So at this point,
opposing counsel has bugged

00:12:46.310 --> 00:12:49.610
Rudy's phone and listen
to the false information

00:12:49.610 --> 00:12:53.540
of him pretending to talk
to one of the jury members,

00:12:53.540 --> 00:12:56.461
and so he is going to
try to voir dire the jury

00:12:56.461 --> 00:12:59.470
and elicit information
about this jury tampering

00:12:59.470 --> 00:13:00.940
that actually didn't happen.

00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:04.050
- Fairly brilliant way to
screw with opposing counsel.

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:06.110
- Did you, Mr. Porter, or did you not

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:08.210
have a phone conversation a few days ago

00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:09.910
with Mr. Rudy Baylor?

00:13:09.910 --> 00:13:10.743
- Hell no.

00:13:12.530 --> 00:13:14.550
- [Leo] I thought you were
gonna give me an honest answer.

00:13:14.550 --> 00:13:16.270
- I gave you an honest answer.

00:13:16.270 --> 00:13:18.901
- [Leo] Are you sure, Mr. Porter?

00:13:18.901 --> 00:13:20.750
- I'm damn sure.

00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:22.914
- Mr. Porter, in a courthouse,

00:13:22.914 --> 00:13:25.230
in a courthouse in the United States,

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:27.500
in a courthouse of justice
in the state of Tennessee,

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:29.530
I say you did not
provide an honest answer.

00:13:29.530 --> 00:13:31.010
- Don't call me a liar.

00:13:31.010 --> 00:13:32.200
- Oh you're a liar all right.
- Mr. Jones.

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:33.968
- You son of a (bleep)

00:13:33.968 --> 00:13:35.491
(yelling)

00:13:35.491 --> 00:13:38.142
- And the bailiff tackled them.

00:13:38.142 --> 00:13:40.970
(yelling)

00:13:40.970 --> 00:13:43.810
- Bailiff, remove Mr.
Porter from this courtroom.

00:13:43.810 --> 00:13:46.790
Mr. Billy Porter, you are
excused from the jury.

00:13:46.790 --> 00:13:49.583
- Yeah, seems like a good
reason to dismiss a juror.

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:53.390
- Your Honor, I move to dismiss

00:13:53.390 --> 00:13:54.853
the entire panel, your Honor.

00:13:56.370 --> 00:13:58.580
- Denied.
(laughs)

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:01.180
- So Rudy fed them false information.

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:04.000
The defense counsel bought
it hook, line, and sinker,

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:05.550
and they made a fool of themselves,

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:07.980
and now the jury probably
hates defense counsel.

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:11.400
So great job by Rudy under
the odd circumstances

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:14.910
of having his office and phone
bugged by opposing counsel.

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:18.720
I don't know if that's the
right tactic to have taken.

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:21.690
If he had taken that
information to the judge,

00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:23.330
Leo Drummond, the defense counsel,

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:27.025
could have been disbarred,
and that would really

00:14:27.025 --> 00:14:31.370
have thrown a wrench
into the entire defense.

00:14:31.370 --> 00:14:33.263
- Please state your name for the record.

00:14:34.280 --> 00:14:36.483
- Mrs. Margerene Black.

00:14:37.896 --> 00:14:41.910
- Now Mrs. Black, you are
the mother of Donny Ray Black

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:44.410
who recently died of
acute myosolidic leukemia

00:14:44.410 --> 00:14:45.810
because the defendant, Great Benefit--

00:14:45.810 --> 00:14:48.163
- Objection, leading.

00:14:48.163 --> 00:14:51.330
- Yeah, common mistake that lawyers make,

00:14:51.330 --> 00:14:53.630
especially on direct examination is that

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:56.688
they try to fit too much into the question

00:14:56.688 --> 00:15:00.040
instead of just letting the
witness tell their story.

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:02.590
On direct examination,
the lawyer should just

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:04.000
disappear into the background,

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:06.040
you're just facilitating the testimony

00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:09.600
and you're asking open-ended
questions like what happened,

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:12.630
how did that make you
feel, what happened next,

00:15:12.630 --> 00:15:13.840
can you explain that?

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:16.160
And he is asking very leading questions,

00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:17.250
shouldn't do that.

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:21.183
- You must be stupid, stupid, stupid.

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:25.830
Sincerely, Everett Leftkin,

00:15:25.830 --> 00:15:27.530
vice president, claims department.

00:15:29.296 --> 00:15:31.400
(sniffles)

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:32.530
- Read it again.

00:15:32.530 --> 00:15:34.900
- Objection, repetitious, your Honor.

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:36.333
- Sustained.
- Correct.

00:15:36.333 --> 00:15:38.300
Not allowed to do that.

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:39.450
- I tender the witness.

00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:41.840
- Mr. Drummond.

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:42.673
- Your Honor,

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:46.373
please remove that exhibit.

00:15:47.860 --> 00:15:51.514
- Oh man, that is, that is so great.

00:15:51.514 --> 00:15:54.820
I've actually never seen
this in a movie before.

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:57.320
It is a common trial tactic to,

00:15:57.320 --> 00:15:58.550
when you have a demonstrative,

00:15:58.550 --> 00:16:01.810
whether it is a projector or a,

00:16:01.810 --> 00:16:05.030
you know, a physical
blow-up of a document,

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:08.961
to leave them up for as
long as you possibly can,

00:16:08.961 --> 00:16:11.530
to have the greatest effect on the jury.

00:16:11.530 --> 00:16:14.570
The experienced trial lawyer,
the fist thing he does

00:16:14.570 --> 00:16:16.631
before he begins his cross-examination

00:16:16.631 --> 00:16:19.960
is he tells the other side to
take down his demonstrative.

00:16:19.960 --> 00:16:23.016
That is possibly the greatest trial detail

00:16:23.016 --> 00:16:25.190
I think I have ever seen in a movie,

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:26.860
it's just, it's perfect.

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:28.010
- Hi this is Deck Shifflet,

00:16:28.010 --> 00:16:30.230
I need to speak to Big
Ryan, can you connect me?

00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:32.200
- [Man] Be right over, just a second.

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:33.033
- Okay.

00:16:33.994 --> 00:16:36.661
(playful music)

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:39.560
- Hello.

00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:40.840
- [Deck] Hey boss, it's Deck.

00:16:40.840 --> 00:16:42.518
- Oh hey Deck, how you doing?

00:16:42.518 --> 00:16:43.522
- [Deck] Good, how are you?

00:16:43.522 --> 00:16:45.653
- Well cool.
- Are you here?

00:16:46.750 --> 00:16:48.310
- Well I'm here and there.

00:16:48.310 --> 00:16:50.158
- Uh, here and there.

00:16:50.158 --> 00:16:53.670
- So in the book version,
Deck Shifflet actually knows

00:16:53.670 --> 00:16:56.262
where Bruiser put all
of his illegal money,

00:16:56.262 --> 00:16:59.230
which is a difference between
the movie and the book,

00:16:59.230 --> 00:17:01.463
which I listened to on Audible.

00:17:03.733 --> 00:17:06.513
- Listen, I got a stolen
evidence situation.

00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:11.077
- Okay, stolen evidence, uh, let's see.

00:17:13.276 --> 00:17:14.850
Uh, the de Soto case.

00:17:14.850 --> 00:17:16.500
- [Deck] De Soto?

00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:21.021
- Carmine de Soto, you
remember him, Club Ruby?

00:17:21.021 --> 00:17:24.238
- Uh-huh, where do I find that?

00:17:24.238 --> 00:17:28.356
- Uh, around '92 you'll
find it, let's see,

00:17:28.356 --> 00:17:31.876
650 something uh, southwest second.

00:17:31.876 --> 00:17:34.120
- [Deck] Boss, you're a life-saver, '92?

00:17:34.120 --> 00:17:35.920
- Yeah, Club Ruby case.

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:38.620
- So that's really funny because
what they are talking about

00:17:38.620 --> 00:17:42.220
is the case citation for
a case that apparently

00:17:42.220 --> 00:17:44.970
has some good authority
on using stolen evidence.

00:17:44.970 --> 00:17:48.276
He said you can find it in 650 something,

00:17:48.276 --> 00:17:51.990
southwest second, and then
he didn't give a pin site,

00:17:51.990 --> 00:17:54.160
but what he is talking about is

00:17:54.160 --> 00:17:58.552
the volume itself, the book
of the compendium of cases,

00:17:58.552 --> 00:18:01.327
all the reported cases, 650 something,

00:18:01.327 --> 00:18:03.940
in the southwest second reporter,

00:18:03.940 --> 00:18:06.507
which is absolutely the right reporter,

00:18:06.507 --> 00:18:08.970
where you will find all of these cases.

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:12.080
That's another thing that you
rarely see in legal movies

00:18:12.080 --> 00:18:15.436
is the correct nomenclature
for finding a case citation.

00:18:15.436 --> 00:18:17.870
- This is the claims
manual that was given to me

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:18.850
by Jackie Lemancyzk.

00:18:18.850 --> 00:18:21.420
- Objection, your Honor, stolen
work papers, inadmissible.

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:22.310
Rule on this.

00:18:22.310 --> 00:18:23.143
- [Judge] Sustained.

00:18:23.143 --> 00:18:24.213
- May we approach?

00:18:29.400 --> 00:18:31.320
- [Leo] I thought this
matter was already settled.

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:33.850
- Your Honor, I just
this morning found a case

00:18:33.850 --> 00:18:36.536
that is controlling in
this factual situation.

00:18:36.536 --> 00:18:37.950
(clattering)

00:18:37.950 --> 00:18:40.540
If you'll just take a look at this ruling.

00:18:40.540 --> 00:18:43.145
It's Club Ruby versus Carmine de Soto,

00:18:43.145 --> 00:18:45.490
copy for your Honor and
one for Mr. Drummond.

00:18:45.490 --> 00:18:48.190
Number 585 southwest second, page 431,

00:18:48.190 --> 00:18:51.770
argued by Bruiser, by J.
Lyman Stone, and it shows

00:18:51.770 --> 00:18:54.761
very clearly that stolen
documents are in fact admissible

00:18:54.761 --> 00:18:56.993
if the lawyers played
no part in the theft.

00:18:58.250 --> 00:19:00.329
- Well according to these headnotes,

00:19:00.329 --> 00:19:02.480
this case will overrule your objection.

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:06.680
- Yeah so the trial court is
not bound by its own prior

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:09.070
rulings, it can revisit its
rulings for the most part.

00:19:09.070 --> 00:19:12.010
This is a great example of
what's called a pocket brief,

00:19:12.010 --> 00:19:14.350
where you will either take the case

00:19:14.350 --> 00:19:17.000
or write a quick argument, write it down

00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:18.560
so that you can provide it to the judge

00:19:18.560 --> 00:19:22.520
when the need arises, and
here he has provided this case

00:19:22.520 --> 00:19:25.120
to the judge to revisit the prior decision

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:27.900
on stolen work product, and
what the judge mentions,

00:19:27.900 --> 00:19:29.680
this is such a great detail too.

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:32.138
The judge didn't read
the actual full case,

00:19:32.138 --> 00:19:34.364
but he read what he called headnotes.

00:19:34.364 --> 00:19:38.180
Headnotes are the summaries
that the case databases

00:19:38.180 --> 00:19:39.929
put at the top of the case

00:19:39.929 --> 00:19:42.837
so you don't have to read
the entire case itself,

00:19:42.837 --> 00:19:45.340
it summarizes the relevant law.

00:19:45.340 --> 00:19:49.290
Such attention to detail, it's incredible.

00:19:49.290 --> 00:19:51.340
- This case will overrule your objection.

00:19:52.260 --> 00:19:54.055
Sorry Leo.
(laughs)

00:19:54.055 --> 00:19:55.855
- Oh I'm sure you are, your Honor,

00:19:55.855 --> 00:19:58.010
but note my strong objection.

00:19:58.010 --> 00:19:59.023
- Objection noted.

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:01.280
- May I approach?

00:20:01.280 --> 00:20:02.930
- Oh and this is another great detail.

00:20:02.930 --> 00:20:06.462
The stenographer stands up from her desk

00:20:06.462 --> 00:20:10.100
so that she can take down
what is being discussed

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:12.453
between the judge and the counsel,

00:20:12.453 --> 00:20:15.940
even though it's not being
projected over the PA system,

00:20:15.940 --> 00:20:18.770
she is still taking down the dialog

00:20:18.770 --> 00:20:21.420
that's going on because that
has to go in the record.

00:20:21.420 --> 00:20:23.520
I don't think I've ever
seen that in a legal movie,

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:25.443
it's just, it's perfect.

00:20:31.590 --> 00:20:33.977
- Mr. Keeley, this a
report from Great Benefit's

00:20:33.977 --> 00:20:37.313
own medical committee, which you chaired.

00:20:39.560 --> 00:20:41.310
Would you please read from line 18.

00:20:44.410 --> 00:20:47.550
- Since bone marrow transplants
have become standard

00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:52.210
procedure, Great Benefit
would be financially justified

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:54.593
in investing in bone marrow clinics.

00:20:56.560 --> 00:20:58.570
- [Rudy] Approach the witness, your Honor.

00:20:58.570 --> 00:20:59.470
- [Judge] You may.

00:21:00.310 --> 00:21:03.780
- [Rudy] Thank you.

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:06.320
There you go, a little louder.

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:08.570
- Objection, your Honor, repetitious.

00:21:08.570 --> 00:21:10.780
- Oh my god, that's so good.

00:21:10.780 --> 00:21:12.610
I think this was done on purpose,

00:21:12.610 --> 00:21:14.740
and I think it's to show that Rudy is

00:21:14.740 --> 00:21:16.970
sort of becoming a great trial lawyer.

00:21:16.970 --> 00:21:19.380
If we rewind it back, in the prior scene,

00:21:19.380 --> 00:21:21.990
he moves the microphone
away with the binder,

00:21:21.990 --> 00:21:24.880
he kind of nudges it away,
making the microphone

00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:26.010
a little bit further away,

00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:29.320
so that when the witness
reads this incredibly

00:21:29.320 --> 00:21:32.933
damning statement, it's
a little low in volume.

00:21:32.933 --> 00:21:35.460
He then walks back to the witness stand,

00:21:35.460 --> 00:21:38.070
moves the microphone towards the witness,

00:21:38.070 --> 00:21:41.214
and asks that the witness
read the statement again

00:21:41.214 --> 00:21:44.220
because it wasn't picked
up the first time,

00:21:44.220 --> 00:21:47.030
but I think the point
there is to show that

00:21:47.030 --> 00:21:50.064
he moved it away on purpose
so that he could get around

00:21:50.064 --> 00:21:53.880
the normal objection to
repetitious testimony.

00:21:53.880 --> 00:21:57.060
I've seen an attorney essentially
do the exact same thing

00:21:58.080 --> 00:22:01.850
when moving the microphone
closer to the witness

00:22:01.850 --> 00:22:04.910
so that they can say it more
clearly for the second time

00:22:04.910 --> 00:22:07.730
to really hammer home this great evidence.

00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:10.938
This is just, this is
amazing, it's amazing.

00:22:10.938 --> 00:22:13.521
(upbeat music)

00:22:14.780 --> 00:22:17.320
All right, that was The Rainmaker,

00:22:17.320 --> 00:22:19.910
one of my favorite legal
movies of all time,

00:22:19.910 --> 00:22:21.960
and I'm glad to see that it stands up,

00:22:21.960 --> 00:22:24.180
but now it's time to
give the movie a grade

00:22:24.180 --> 00:22:27.570
for legal realism.
(gavel bangs)

00:22:27.570 --> 00:22:31.610
On the one hand, we have a
very, very realistic trial.

00:22:31.610 --> 00:22:34.440
We have trial tactics that not only exist

00:22:34.440 --> 00:22:38.399
in the real world, but this
movie captures nuances that

00:22:38.399 --> 00:22:42.810
I've just never seen in any
other work of legal fiction.

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:47.304
On the other side, there are
some bits of dramatic license.

00:22:47.304 --> 00:22:50.228
You have bad lawyers, but frankly,

00:22:50.228 --> 00:22:53.670
they're bad lawyers within
the narrative of the story,

00:22:53.670 --> 00:22:55.620
so they should be behaving badly.

00:22:55.620 --> 00:22:57.690
So that actually seems to make sense.

00:22:57.690 --> 00:23:00.050
All in all, I think this might be

00:23:00.050 --> 00:23:03.130
the most accurate legal
drama that I've ever seen.

00:23:03.130 --> 00:23:05.780
So I'm giving The Rainmaker an A+.

00:23:05.780 --> 00:23:08.020
I had no idea it was directed
by Francis Ford Coppola.

00:23:08.020 --> 00:23:09.230
It's just beautiful.

00:23:09.230 --> 00:23:10.360
- I guess that's it.

00:23:10.360 --> 00:23:12.500
- Did you know that John
Grisham says this movie

00:23:12.500 --> 00:23:15.280
is the best adaptation
of any of his books?

00:23:15.280 --> 00:23:18.300
It's easy to see why, because
this movie is fantastic,

00:23:18.300 --> 00:23:19.810
but the book is almost as good.

00:23:19.810 --> 00:23:21.400
I wanted to see what the differences were

00:23:21.400 --> 00:23:22.440
between the movie and the book,

00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:24.110
so I prepared for this review by listening

